MAME drawshm: a DRIVER-AGNOSTIC `-video shm` OSD render module.

WHY

The gallery already has a shared-memory frame producer, and it converts exactly
one tile. `mame-newport-shm-framebuffer.patch` publishes finished frames out of
`src/devices/bus/gio64/newport.cpp` -- the SGI Indy's graphics device. No other
machine in MAME has a Newport, so nine keyboard-only MAME exhibits and every
future MAME one are still stuck on the bridge: a QEMU/KVM VM running a bare X
kiosk whose only job is to hold one full-screen emulator window that streamhost
then screen-scrapes.

The consumer half is already generic (streamhost `SH_CAPTURE=shm`,
streamhost/streamhost/src/capture/shm.rs) and so is the input half
(`mame-ctlsock.patch`, an OSD-level module parameterised by environment). The
missing piece was a driver-agnostic frame PRODUCER. This is it.

WHERE, AND WHY THERE

At the OSD render layer, beside `drawnone` and `drawsdl` -- not against any
video device. A module there sees the POST-RENDER TARGET: the primitive list
MAME has already composed for the output surface, for any machine, at any size
it is asked for. The module names no machine, no driver and no device; every
type it touches is `osd_window`, `render_target` or `render_primitive_list`.
That is the whole tier-level claim, and it is checkable by grep.

ARBITRARY OUTPUT RESOLUTION is a requirement, not a nicety. A host-native arm
is only comparable with a bridge arm if both produce the same pixel count: a
bridge kiosk root is 800x600 (1024x768 on some suites) while a vintage
machine's native raster may be 256x192, and comparing those two measures the
RESOLUTION, not the bridge. `MAME_SHM_SIZE=WxH` sets the published surface
directly and MAME's own aspect handling letterboxes the machine inside it.

CONFIGURATION is by environment, exactly like the ctlsock module, so one binary
serves every machine with no per-machine code and no rebuild:

  MAME_SHM_PATH   (required)  file to publish into; the consumer mmaps it
  MAME_SHM_SIZE   (optional)  "WxH" surface; default = the window size, which
                              is MAME's own -resolution when one was given and
                              the machine's minimum bounds when it was not
  MAME_SHM_TRACE  (optional)  log the mapping and a periodic publish count

WIRE FORMAT is the one streamhost already speaks -- a 64-byte header (magic
'IFB1', version, width, height, stride, bpp, u64 seqlock, dirty rect) followed
by width*height host-endian XRGB8888 pixels, i.e. B,G,R,X in memory, which is
byte-identical to the BGRA the encoder consumes. Nothing on this path converts
a pixel; the `software_renderer<uint32_t, 0,0,0, 16,8,0>` instantiation is what
buys that, and it is the same one drawsdl selects for an rmask of 0x00ff0000.
This patch does NOT invent a second contract and changes nothing on the Rust
side.

WHAT IT DOES NOT GET, AND WHY THAT IS THE RIGHT TRADE

Damage. The Newport producer reuses the whole-frame render cache's dirty bit
and publishes an EMPTY dirty rect for an unchanged frame, so an idle exhibit
costs the consumer nothing. A render-layer module has no such information --
the primitive list carries none -- so every published frame is flagged
whole-frame dirty and streamhost's own frame diff (`SH_SHM_DAMAGE=1`, the
default) derives the real bounding box. That diff runs on the CONSUMER's core,
not the emulator's, which is the right side of the trade for an emulator that
is CPU-bound. It is also why this patch does NOT replace the Newport one:
irix keeps its optimised special case.

CONTAINMENT

  * Freestanding. It touches only files no tile-specific patch in
    scripts/build-guests/irix/irix-mame-stack.sh touches (`modules.lua`,
    `osdobj_common.cpp`, plus its own new `src/osd/modules/render/drawshm.cpp`),
    so it dry-run-applies to a pristine tree and any MAME tile inherits it by
    adding one line to a build.
  * Registered AFTER `RENDERER_NONE`, so `-video auto` can never fall through
    to it (`none` always initialises) and the module is reachable only by
    asking for it by name. Zero change to any existing selection.
  * No timers, no save items: it cannot change a savestate signature and
    therefore cannot orphan a golden.
  * `-video shm` with `MAME_SHM_PATH` unset is a LOUD failure, not a blank
    exhibit.
  * One publisher per mapping: with `-numscreens > 1` window 0 publishes and
    the others render nothing, so the seqlock's single-producer premise holds.

PROVEN (2026-08-10, lab box, headless, no X server and no window)

MAME's Dragon 32 -- `dragon32` out of `src/mame/trs/dragon.cpp`, a machine with
no relation to the SGI Indy -- built from upstream 8f21e978 with THIS PATCH AS
THE ONLY CHANGE (`make SUBTARGET=dragon SOURCES=src/mame/trs/dragon.cpp`,
binary md5 8fc95dacadd287e2968f881a00bbff64). MAME's own option help then reads
"-video  video output method: opengl, bgfx, accel, soft, none or shm".

Each run: `-video shm -sound none -skip_gameinfo -ext "" -seconds_to_run 12`
with DISPLAY and SDL_VIDEODRIVER unset, so there is no X server and no window
and the run ends by itself. The mapping was read back with the exhibit's
EXISTING consumer-side dumper -- scripts/build-guests/irix/irix-bench/shmpng.py,
unmodified, md5 87b075929c097d35240ebca30bb65f8b in the repo, in the box
checkout and in the work dir -- which is itself the contract check: a tool
written for the Newport producer reads this one with no edit. Census by
scripts/dev/frame-compare.py --frame:

  MAME_SHM_SIZE   mapping bytes   geometry   colours  entropy  non-dominant px
  <unset>           458,316       391x293       3     1.0520   51,648  (45.08%)
  800x600         1,920,064       800x600       3     1.0512   216,700 (45.15%)
  320x240           307,264       320x240       3     1.0554   34,540  (44.97%)

Every byte count is exactly 64 + w*h*4. All three clear the emptiness floor
(>= 1000 non-dominant pixels) by 34x or more, so a black or flooded frame could
not have passed. All three carry the SAME three colours -- #30d200 raster,
#263016 MC6847 border, #007c00 text -- and those are the same three the
`dragon32` BRIDGE tile's own screenshot carries, and the Dragon's boot text
("(C) 1982 DRAGON DATA LTD / 16K BASIC INTERPRETER 1.0 / (C) 1982 BY MICROSOFT
/ OK") is legible in all three.

The scaling is size-independent: the green raster occupies 0.688 x 0.655 of the
surface at 320x240, at 391x293 and at 800x600 alike -- and 0.688 x 0.655 is
also what MAME's OWN native snapshot (`machine.video:snapshot()`, a code path
that never touches an OSD renderer) measures.

And the frame is right to the pixel. Run at MAME's own native target geometry
with `-nokeepaspect` so the two are directly comparable, the published mapping
versus `video:snapshot()` FROM THE SAME RUN differs in 96 pixels inside one
8x12 box -- one Dragon character cell, the blinking BASIC cursor, two different
frames apart. frame-compare's verdict is LOCALISED.
