+// license:BSD-3-Clause
+// copyright-holders:osgallery lab
+//============================================================
+//
+//  drawshm.cpp - publish the post-render target into a shared-memory
+//                framebuffer (`-video shm`)
+//
+//============================================================
+//
+//  WHAT THIS IS, AND WHY IT IS AT THE OSD RENDER LAYER
+//
+//  The gallery's IRIX exhibit already publishes finished frames into a
+//  file-backed mapping instead of pushing them through SDL + llvmpipe + an X
+//  server, which measured +41% emulation speed and delivered the exact
+//  emulated framebuffer instead of a window-scaled resample. But that producer
+//  is a patch to src/devices/bus/gio64/newport.cpp -- the SGI Indy's graphics
+//  device. No other machine has a Newport, so it converts exactly ONE tile and
+//  nothing else.
+//
+//  This module is the driver-agnostic form of the same idea. It sits where
+//  drawnone and drawsdl sit, so it sees the POST-RENDER TARGET: the primitive
+//  list MAME has already composed for the output surface, for ANY machine, at
+//  ANY size we ask for. It contains no reference to a machine, a driver or a
+//  video device, and it never can -- everything it touches is osd_window,
+//  render_target and render_primitive_list.
+//
+//  The cost model differs from the Newport producer in exactly one place, and
+//  it is worth stating rather than discovering: the device-level producer gets
+//  a DAMAGE FLAG for free (it reuses the whole-frame render cache's dirty bit),
+//  and a render-layer module cannot -- the primitive list carries no such
+//  thing. So every published frame is flagged whole-frame dirty and the
+//  consumer's own frame diff (streamhost SH_SHM_DAMAGE=1, the default) derives
+//  the real bounding box. That diff runs on the CONSUMER's core, not the
+//  emulator's, which is the right side of the trade for an emulator that is
+//  CPU-bound.
+//
+//  ARBITRARY OUTPUT RESOLUTION is a hard requirement, not a nicety. A
+//  host-native arm is only comparable with a bridge arm if both produce the
+//  same pixel count: a bridge kiosk root is 800x600 while a vintage machine's
+//  native raster may be 256x192, and comparing those two measures the
+//  RESOLUTION, not the bridge. MAME_SHM_SIZE sets the published surface size
+//  directly; the render target is asked for exactly that many pixels and MAME's
+//  own aspect handling letterboxes the machine into it.
+//
+//  CONFIGURATION is by environment, exactly like the ctlsock module, so one
+//  binary serves every machine with no per-machine code and no rebuild:
+//
+//    MAME_SHM_PATH  (required)  file to publish into; the consumer mmaps it
+//    MAME_SHM_SIZE  (optional)  "WxH" output surface; default = the window size
+//                               (which is MAME's own -resolution, or the
+//                               machine's minimum bounds when that is "auto")
+//    MAME_SHM_TRACE (optional)  set to log mapping + a periodic publish count
+//
+//  WIRE FORMAT -- identical to the one streamhost's SH_CAPTURE=shm consumer
+//  already speaks (streamhost/streamhost/src/capture/shm.rs). This module does
+//  NOT invent a second contract:
+//
+//    off  type  field
+//      0  u32   magic 'IFB1' (0x31424649 little-endian)
+//      4  u32   version (1)
+//      8  u32   width
+//     12  u32   height
+//     16  u32   stride (bytes per row; always width*4 here)
+//     20  u32   bpp (32)
+//     24  u64   sequence (seqlock)
+//     32  u32   dirty_x0    36 u32 dirty_y0
+//     40  u32   dirty_x1    44 u32 dirty_y1
+//     48        pad to 64
+//     64        width*height pixels
+//
+//  Pixels are host-endian XRGB8888 -- B,G,R,X in memory on a little-endian
+//  host, byte-identical to the BGRA the encoder consumes, so nothing on this
+//  path converts a pixel. That is what the software_renderer instantiation
+//  below buys: <uint32_t, 0,0,0, 16,8,0> puts red at bit 16, green at 8, blue
+//  at 0, which is exactly the layout drawsdl selects for an rmask of
+//  0x00ff0000.
+//
+//  SYNCHRONISATION is a seqlock: one producer (MAME's video thread, which is
+//  the only caller of draw()), any number of readers, readers never write. The
+//  sequence goes ODD before pixels are touched and EVEN after, both with
+//  release ordering. A reader takes it with acquire, copies, re-reads, and
+//  retries on odd-or-changed, so a torn frame can never be accepted.
+//
+//  ONE PUBLISHER PER MAPPING. With -numscreens > 1 every window would
+//  otherwise write the same file and the seqlock's single-producer premise
+//  would be false. Window 0 publishes; the others render nothing, exactly like
+//  -video none.
+//
+//============================================================
+
